L1: Proteome-Driven Elucidation of Adaptive Responses
L2: to Combined Vitamin E and C Deficiency in Zebrafish
L3: Journal of Proteome Research (2015)
L4: Ievgen Motorykin, Maret
L5: G. Traber, Robert L. Tanguay, Claudia S. Maier
L6: † Department
L7: of Chemistry,  Oregon State University , 153 Gilbert Hall, Corvallis, Oregon 97331,  United States
L8: ‡ Linus
L9: Pauling Institute and School of Biological and Population Health Sciences,  Oregon State University , 307 Linus Pauling Science Center, Corvallis, Oregon 97331,  United States
L10: § Department
L11: of Environmental and Molecular Toxicology,  Oregon State University , 1007 Agriculture & Life Sciences Building, Corvallis, Oregon 97331,  United States
L12: The purpose of this study was to determine the system-wide consequences
L13: of deficiencies in two essential micronutrients, vitamins E and C,
L14: on the proteome using zebrafish ( Danio rerio ) as
L15: one of the few vertebrate models that similar to humans cannot synthesize
L16: vitamin C. We describe a label-free proteomics workflow to detect
L17: changes in protein abundance estimates dependent on vitamin regimes.
L18: We used ion-mobility-enhanced data-independent tandem mass spectrometry
L19: to determine differential regulation of proteins in response to low
L20: dietary levels of vitamin C with or without vitamin E. The detection
L21: limit of the method was as low as 20 amol, and the dynamic range was
L22: five orders of magnitude for the protein-level estimates. On the basis
L23: of the quantitative changes obtained, we built a network of protein
L24: interactions that reflect the whole organism’s response to
L25: vitamin C deficiency. The proteomics-driven study revealed that in
L26: vitamin-E-deficient fish, vitamin C deficiency is associated with
L27: induction of stress response, astrogliosis, and a shift from glycolysis
L28: to glutaminolysis as an alternative mechanism to satisfy cellular
L29: energy requirements.
L30: Article
L31: vitamin C; vitamin E; ion mobility; MS E; label-free quantification; zebrafish; glycolysis; glutaminolysis
L32: Introduction
L33: The purpose of this study was to determine the consequences of
L34: deficiencies in dietary antioxidants, vitamins C and E, on the zebrafish
L35: proteome composition. The combination of deficiencies was used because
L36: this dietary manipulation is known to cause severe abnormalities. 1 , 2  Protein abundance estimates were obtained by label-free accurate
L37: quantification based on data-independent (MS E ) acquisition.
L38: Functional interpretation of the systems-wide proteome responses revealed
L39: the biomolecular networks affected by vitamin C and E deficiency.
L40: In zebrafish, the lack of vitamin E potentiates vitamin C deficiency
L41: and causes degenerative myopathy. 3  The
L42: classical roles of vitamin C (ascorbic acid, AA) are in maintaining
L43: redox homeostasis 4 − 6  and facilitating the recycling of vitamin E. 7 , 8  More recently, vitamin C has been identified as an essential cofactor
L44: for multiple enzymes involved in the hydroxylation of procollagen 9 , 10  and several dioxygenases that are involved in maintaining endothelial
L45: function with relevance to atherosclerosis 11  and sepsis. 12 , 13  Inadequate vitamin C levels,
L46: potentially leading to hypovitaminosis, remain widespread health concerns
L47: in humans affecting particularly underprivileged populations. Recent
L48: research indicates that smoking 14  and Western
L49: diets rich in fat and cholesterol 15  exacerbate
L50: vitamin C deficiency.
L51: In most animals, vitamin C is synthesized in the liver or kidneys
L52: and transferred to tissues via circulation. 16 , 17  Humans are not capable of synthesizing ascorbic acid due to the
L53: lack of the functional gene for  l -gulonolactone oxidase. 18 , 19  For studying the effect of vitamin C deficiency, the most widely
L54: used animal model is the guinea pig that similarly to humans cannot
L55: synthesize vitamin C. 20  The gulonolactone
L56: oxidase knockout (Gulo –/– ) mouse is an established
L57: transgenic animal model for deciphering the physiological roles of
L58: vitamin C. 21 , 22  Similarly to humans, zebrafish
L59: also cannot synthesize vitamin C. 23 − 28  A first report using MS-based metabolomics to study the effect of
L60: vitamin C deficiency in zebrafish revealed that vitamin C deficiency
L61: affects the purine metabolism. 29  A classical
L62: antioxidant function of vitamin C entails the recycling of the tocopheroxyl
L63: radical that is generated when vitamin E (α-tocopherol, α-T)
L64: functions as a peroxyl radical, chain-breaking antioxidant. 30 , 31  To evaluate the interrelating roles of vitamins C and E, we used
L65: a 2 × 2 design in the present study resulting in four dietary
L66: groups, namely, vitamin-E- and -C-adequate (E+C+), vitamin-E- and
L67: -C-inadequate (E–C−), vitamin-C-adequate but inadequate
L68: in vitamin E (E–C+), and vitamin-C-inadequate but adequate
L69: in vitamin E (E+C−). We subsequently applied a label-free comparative
L70: proteomics approach for examining the systems-wide consequences of
L71: the different vitamin regimes in adult zebrafish.
L72: In a typical “bottom-up” shotgun experiment, a protein
L73: mixture is proteolytically digested with a protease, such as trypsin,
L74: to create peptides for further analysis. Liquid chromatography in
L75: combination with tandem mass spectrometry (LC–MS/MS) has become
L76: the predominantly used technique to both qualitatively and quantitatively
L77: analyze a complex mixture of peptides. 32 − 34  The peptides are chromatographically
L78: separated using reversed-phase (RP) chromatography and subsequently
L79: subjected to collision-induced dissociation (CID) in a tandem mass
L80: spectrometer. The product ion spectra are searched against protein
L81: sequence databases to identify the peptides. Unlike comparative isotope-coded
L82: labeling methods (e.g., ICAT or iTRAQ), label-free mass spectrometry-based
L83: quantitative proteomic approaches are based on the fact that in electrospray
L84: ionization (ESI) the peak signal intensity is linearly proportional
L85: to the concentration of a peptide (over an approximate range of 5–5000
L86: fmol). 35 , 36  Currently, two acquisition techniques have
L87: been technically realized and applied for detecting and quantifying
L88: peptides, namely, data-dependent acquisitions (DDAs) and data-independent
L89: acquisitions (DIAs).
L90: Data-dependent acquisitions are based on tracking of precursor
L91: ion intensities on-the-go. 37 The peptide
L92: precursor ions that fulfill preselected features are sequentially
L93: subjected to MS/MS during the time course of the chromatography. Proteolytic
L94: digests in proteomics-type workflow are often complex and even when
L95: using ultraperformance liquid chromatography (UPLC) at every moment
L96: multiple peptides coelute. In DDA techniques, survey scan and MS/MS
L97: scans are used sequentially and compete for duty cycle time. Therefore,
L98: both the number of the most intense peaks subjected to MS/MS analysis
L99: and the timespan of a survey scan need to be optimized to acquire
L100: accurate mass information for the peptide precursor ions and to ensure
L101: information-rich fragment ion spectra. 38 , 39  Albeit somewhat
L102: dependent on the mass-spectrometry platform used, a possible caveat
L103: of DDAs is that a substantial portion of the acquisition cycle is
L104: spent acquiring fragment ion spectra; therefore, the intensities of
L105: the peptide ions are measured inaccurately and, consequently, are
L106: less suited for estimating peptide abundances. 39
L107: DIA techniques perform parallel fragmentation of precursor ions,
L108: meaning that all peptide precursors are fragmented simultaneously
L109: regardless of their characteristics. For proteomic-type applications,
L110: currently most DIAs are realized by coupling nanoUPLC systems to high-resolution
L111: quadrupole time-of-flight (Q-TOF) mass spectrometers. This combination
L112: of equipment takes advantage of the high peak and separation capacity
L113: of UPLCs and the measurement of precursor and product ions with accurate
L114: mass. In the DIA mode, the Q-TOF mass spectrometer conducts a low-energy
L115: MS scan providing access to precursor ion mass and intensity data
L116: for quantitation and an elevated energy scan to obtain product ion
L117: information. A critical post acquisition step of the DIA technique
L118: is the alignment of the fragment ions with the respective precursor
L119: ion. DIA-based acquisitions enable quantification based on the peptide
L120: ion intensities. The estimation of protein abundance is based on the
L121: observation that the average signal intensity of the three most intense
L122: tryptic peptides relates to the protein abundance level regardless
L123: of protein size. 40  The estimation of protein
L124: levels (moles of each identified protein) is based on using an internal
L125: protein standard (spiked in at a defined amount).
L126: Recently, a commercially available hybrid high-resolution ion mobility
L127: quadrupole time-of-flight (IM-Q-TOF) mass spectrometry system that
L128: enables the acquisition of ion mobility-enhanced DIA (aka IM-MS E ) data has become available. 41  In
L129: this instrument configuration, ions are separated according to their
L130: velocity with which they transverse the traveling wave ion mobility
L131: device. Parameters that affect the ion’s mobility are its charge,
L132: size, and shape. 42  In ion mobility-enhanced
L133: MS E  acquisitions, ions are separated according to their
L134: mobility and then dependent on the energy regime applied in the transfer
L135: region directly passed on to the TOF analyzer or collisionally dissociated,
L136: and the resulting fragment ions are measured in the TOF analyzer.
L137: In the IM-MS E  mode, fragment ions are drift time-aligned
L138: with their precursors. An often touted advantage of ion mobility-enhanced
L139: MS E  acquisitions is the orthogonal separation space gained
L140: additionally to the chromatographic separation of the peptides prior
L141: to collision-induced dissociation leading to spectral decongestion,
L142: improved peptide detection, and dynamic range, all leading cooperatively
L143: to more protein identifications. 43
L144: To investigate the consequences of the combination of vitamin E
L145: and C deficiency on protein networks in zebrafish, we profiled the
L146: changes in protein abundances. The observed proteome changes were
L147: then statistically evaluated and functionally annotated. The protein
L148: expression data were used for constructing a protein interaction network.
L149: Subnetworks were extracted that comprised proteins related to stress
L150: response, glycolysis, and TCA cycles. The observed changes in protein
L151: abundances for zebrafish deficient in vitamins E and C suggest a metabolic
L152: switch from glycolysis to glutaminolysis as a means of alternative
L153: energy production.
L154: Materials and Methods
L155: Chemicals
L156: Dithiolthreitol (DTT) and iodoacetamide were
L157: purchased from Bio-Rad (Hercules, CA). MS-grade water, acetonitrile,
L158: and formic acid (99%) were obtained from EMD Chemicals (Gibbstown,
L159: NJ). Sequencing-grade trypsin, resuspension buffer, and protease MAX
L160: solution were purchased from Promega (Madison, WI). [Glu 1 ]-fibrinopeptide B ([Glu 1 ]-Fib) and  Saccharomyces
L161: cerevisiae  enolase digest were obtained from Waters (Milford,
L162: MA). PBS solution was purchased from Fisher (Fair Lawn, NJ).
L163: Fish Husbandry
L164: Housing of the wild-type tropical 5D
L165: strain zebrafish was carried out in the Sinnhuber Aquatic Research
L166: Laboratory at Oregon State University, Corvallis, Oregon. The study
L167: was performed according to protocols approved by the Institutional
L168: Animal Care and Use Committee (IACUC). 44
L169: Feeding Experiment
L170: The experiment was designed to allow
L171: analyzing changes to the proteome upon vitamin C supplementation of
L172: fish that were vitamin-E-adequate or -deficient. To do that, we fed
L173: fish with predefined diets as previously described. 29  In brief, at 42 days of age, 40 fish were divided in two
L174: groups and fed diets low in vitamin C (C–, 50 mg AA/kg diet,
L175: added as Stay-C, DSM Nutritional Products, Parsippany, NJ) with vitamin
L176: E at sufficient levels (E+, 178 μmol RRR-α-tocopherol/kg
L177: diet) or deficient levels (E–, 22 μmol RRR-α-tocopherol/kg
L178: diet). Thus, two vitamin groups were created: E+C– and E–C–
L179: with 20 fish in each group. Induction of vitamin C deficiency continued
L180: for 56 days, after which half of the fish population in each group
L181: was harvested. The diet of remaining fish was altered to have a high
L182: amount of vitamin C (C+, 350 mg AA/kg diet, added as Stay-C), thus
L183: creating another two vitamin groups: E+C+ and E–C+. After an
L184: additional 21 days, the fish were harvested. Thus, four groups with
L185: 10 fish in each were created, each having different vitamins levels
L186: that varied with supplementation: E+C+, E–C+, E+C–,
L187: and E–C– ( Supporting Information , Figure S1). Vitamin E and C concentrations have been published. 29
L188: Protein Extraction and Digestion
L189: Each sample consisted
L190: of one whole flash-frozen fish. The whole flash-frozen fish was individually
L191: ground under liquid nitrogen using a mortar and pestle. The pipetting
L192: scheme used for preparing the protein extracts and digests for each
L193: sample is provided in the  Supporting Information , Table S1. While still dry, the fish powder was transferred to a
L194: microcentrifuge tube and weighed ( Supporting Information , Table S1, column 2). A PBS solution with ProteaseMAX (0.04%) was
L195: added to the microtubes for the extraction of proteins; assuming the
L196: density as 1 mg/mL, its volume was adjusted to six times the fish
L197: weight ( Supporting Information , Table S1,
L198: column 5). The tubes containing the fish powder suspensions were submitted
L199: to three freeze–thaw cycles that included flash-freezing in
L200: liquid nitrogen for 2 min, thawing for 5 min, and sonicating for 15
L201: min. After lysis, tubes were centrifuged at 4 °C for 10 min at
L202: 15 000 relative centrifugal force (rcf), and the supernatants
L203: were transferred to new microcentrifuge tubes (1.3 mL) while solid
L204: residue was discarded. The sample preparation workflow is shown in  Supporting Information , Figure S2.
L205: The
L206: concentration of proteins in each sample was determined by a photometric
L207: (Bradford) assay ( Supporting Information , Table S1, column 6). Proteins in each sample were digested according
L208: to the manufacturer’s protocol (Promega). The pipetting scheme
L209: for preparing the digest of each sample is outlined in  Supporting Information , Table S1, columns 7–9.
L210: In brief, a volume of solution containing 50 μg of proteins
L211: was taken to 93.5 μL with a freshly prepared 50 mM NH 4 HCO 3 . Next, 1 μL of 0.5 M DTT (in Millipore water)
L212: was added to each vial, and solutions were incubated at 56 °C
L213: for 20 min. Then, 2.7 μL of 0.55 M iodoacetamide (in 50 mM NH 4 HCO 3 ) was added, and solutions were incubated at
L214: room temperature in the dark for 15 min. For protein digestion, 1
L215: μL of 1% ProteaseMAX Surfactant in (in 50 mM NH 4 HCO 3 ) and 1.8 μL of trypsin (1 μg/μL in 50 mM
L216: acetic acid) were added. Solutions were incubated at 37 °C for
L217: 3 h. The final volume of each solution of digested proteins was 100
L218: μL. Solutions were centrifuged at 12 000 rcf for 10 s,
L219: and trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) was added at a final concentration
L220: of 0.5%. After snap-freezing in liquid nitrogen, samples were stored
L221: at −20 °C until analyzed. At the end, we conducted LC–IM–MS E  analyses of 8, 10, 10, and 9 samples of groups E+C+, E–C+,
L222: E+C–, and E–C–, respectively ( Supporting Information , Table S1).
L223: Liquid Chromatography–Mass Spectrometry
L224: The
L225: analysis of all samples was performed using a Synapt G2 hybrid quadrupole
L226: time-of-flight mass spectrometer (Waters, Milford, MA) controlled
L227: by MassLynx 4.2 software (Waters). The sample peptide solution (9
L228: μL) was mixed with 1 μL of internal standard ( Saccharomyces cerevisiae  enolase digest, 1 pmol/μL).
L229: With 1 μL injection volume the amount of the internal standard
L230: per injection on column was 100 fmol. Peptides were separated with
L231: a nanoAcquity Ultra Performance LC system (Waters, Milford, MA) equipped
L232: with 100 μm × 100 mm BEH130 C18 column with a particle
L233: size of 1.7 μm (Waters, Milford, MA). The mobile phase A consisted
L234: of 0.1% formic acid in water, and B consisted of 0.1% formic acid
L235: in acetonitrile. Each sample was first retained on a trapping column
L236: and then washed using 99.5% A for 3 min at a flow rate 5 μL/min.
L237: Peptides were separated using a 120 min gradient (3–40% B for
L238: 90 min, 40–90% for 2 min, 90% B for 1 min, 90–3% B for
L239: 2 min, and 3% B for 25 min) and then electrosprayed into the mass
L240: spectrometer, fitted with a nanoSpray source, at a flow rate of 400
L241: nL/min. External calibration of the TOF analyzer was performed using
L242: NaI solution over the range of  m / z  50 to 2000. The instrument operated in positive V-mode over the
L243: calibration range. Mass spectra were acquired in the MS E  mode alternating between a low energy scan (6 eV) to acquire peptide
L244: precursor data and a high-energy scan (ramping from 27 to 50 eV) to
L245: acquire fragmentation data. The capillary voltage was 2.5 kV, and
L246: the source temperature was 40 °C. Scan time was 1.25 s. The instrument
L247: settings are listed in Table S2 ( Supporting Information ). An auxiliary pump delivered a [Glu 1 ]-Fib solution as
L248: an external calibrant (lock-mass) with a concentration of 500 fmol/μL
L249: at a rate of 0.2 μL/min. For lock mass acquisition, a low-energy
L250: scan was acquired for 0.75 s every 60 s throughout a run.
L251: Peak Detection
L252: Elevated energy mass spectra were extracted,
L253: charge-state-deconvoluted, deisotoped, and lock-mass-corrected with
L254: [Glu 1 ]-Fib (MH + m / z  785.8426). All ion mobility-MS E  samples were analyzed
L255: using Identity E  by ProteinLynx Global Server (PLGS) version
L256: 2.5 (Waters Corporation, Milford, MA). The following processing parameters
L257: and their respective settings were used: chromatographic peak width
L258: and MS TOF resolution, automatic; low-energy threshold, 100 counts;
L259: elevated energy threshold, 10 counts; intensity threshold, 750 counts.
L260: Protein Database Search
L261: Waters’ Identity E  was configured to search  Danio rerio  protein
L262: database (26 812 entries) with the digestion enzyme trypsin.
L263: The database was modified by adding the yeast enolase sequence (ENO1_YEAST,
L264: accession number P00924, Uniprot) that was spiked in as internal standard
L265: to facilitate label-free quantification. Identity E  searched
L266: the protein database with a fragment ion mass tolerance of 0.025 Da
L267: and a parent ion tolerance of 0.0100 Da. The iodoacetamide derivative
L268: of cysteine and the oxidation of methionine were specified as variable
L269: modifications of amino acids in Identity E .
L270: Protein Identification, Quantification, and Validation
L271: Scaffold (version 3.3.1, Proteome Software, Portland, OR) was used
L272: to validate MS E -based peptide and protein identifications.
L273: Peptide probabilities were assigned by the Peptide Prophet algorithm. 45  Protein probabilities were assigned by the Protein
L274: Prophet algorithm. 46  Proteins that contained
L275: similar peptides and could not be differentiated based on MS/MS analysis
L276: alone were grouped to satisfy the principles of parsimony. The peptide
L277: and protein probabilities were adjusted to allow the best quantification
L278: of proteins and keeping the false identification rates at low levels.
L279: For each sample, the numbers of peptides assigned with high confidence
L280: are compiled in Table S3 ( Supporting Information ). In addition,  Supporting Information  Table S5 compiles proteins identified, unique peptides assigned
L281: per protein, and protein sequence coverage. This information was directly
L282: obtained from Scaffold.
L283: The average of intensity of the three
L284: most intense peptides of each protein was normalized to that of the
L285: internal standard with an assigned amount of 100 fmol. Proteins could
L286: not be quantified if they ambiguously shared peptides that cannot
L287: be uniquely attributed to one protein. Ultimately, the information
L288: about group name, sample name, protein ID, and quantitative values
L289: for proteins was acquired from Scaffold and exported to spreadsheets.
L290: The data were then imported into Perseus ( http://www.perseus-framework.org/ ) and statistically analyzed. Perseus was developed by the Max Planck
L291: Institute of Biochemistry, Martinsried, Germany and designed to perform
L292: all downstream bioinformatics and statistics on proteomics output
L293: tables.
L294: Western Blot
L295: Three samples were randomly selected from
L296: each group. Samples were prepared for Western Blot analyses by diluting
L297: with sample buffer (5% mercaptoethanol in Laemmli buffer, Bio-Rad,
L298: Hercules, CA) to a concentration of 1 mg/mL of protein. For SDS-PAGE
L299: analysis, 20 μg (20 μL) of proteins was loaded into a
L300: precasted 10% gel (Bio-Rad). After electrotransfer to a nitrocellulose
L301: (NC) membrane (Bio-Rad), the latter was blocked overnight in 5% nonfat
L302: milk in TBS-T (10 mM Tris, pH 8, 150 mM NaCl, 0.05% Tween). The NC
L303: paper was blotted for 1 h with antibodies (Thermo, Rockford, IL) against
L304: pyruvate kinase M2 (Pkm2), glutamate dehydrogenase (Glud1), and glial
L305: fibrillary acidic protein (Gfap). A horseradish-peroxidase-labeled
L306: antigoat and antirabbit IgG (Bio-Rad) and an enhanced chemoluminescence
L307: kit (SuperSignal West Pico Chemiluminescent Substrate, Thermo) were
L308: used to detect signals on a film (Kodak, Rochester, NY). The molecular
L309: mass of a protein was estimated using a protein molecular standard
L310: (Bio-Rad).
L311: Network Construction
L312: To construct the vitamin-C-deficiency
L313: network, protein IDs were uploaded to STRING, Search Tool for the
L314: Retrieval of Interacting Genes ( www.string-db.org ). 47  The data found on protein interactions and the
L315: scores information were extracted. The protein network was constructed
L316: based on protein interactions using Cytoscape 2.8.2 (The Cytoscape
L317: Consortium). The latter is an open-source platform for complex network
L318: analysis and visualization. 48
L319: Results and Discussion
L320: Overall Design and Strategy Applied for the Label-Free Quantitative
L321: Proteomic Study
L322: The goal of this study was to investigate
L323: the consequences of vitamin E and C deficiency on the zebrafish proteome. The analysis and validation of quantitative bottom-up shotgun proteomics
L324: data sets depend critically on the optimal combination of data processing
L325: software tools because each commercially available software package
L326: can perform only a few steps of the data analysis pipeline (Figure  1 ). Here we implemented MassLynx and PLGS for peak
L327: extraction, alignment, and database search; Scaffold for spectra validation
L328: and protein quantitation; Perseus for statistical analysis; and STRING
L329: and Cytoscape for protein interactions analysis and network construction,
L330: respectively.
L331: Figure 1 Data analysis workflow for label-free quantitative proteomics using
L332: LC–IMS–MS E  acquisitions. We implemented MassLynx
L333: and PLGS for peak extraction, alignment, and database search; Scaffold
L334: for spectra validation and protein quantitation; Perseus for statistical
L335: analysis; and STRING and Cytoscape for protein interactions analysis
L336: and network construction.
L337: Effects on Body Weight and Growth Rate
L338: The experimental
L339: design of the feeding study afforded that zebrafish of the E+C–
L340: and E–C– groups were harvested after 98 days and the
L341: E–C+ and E+C+ groups were sacrificed after 119 days. We therefore
L342: expected that the different groups would result in different median
L343: fish body weights. Indeed, as shown in the  Supporting
L344: Information , Figure S3A, the median fish weights were lower
L345: for E+C– and E–C–groups compared with E–C+
L346: and E+C+ groups. The ANOVA test between all four groups did not show
L347: significant results, but the median body weights were significantly
L348: different between the E+C+ and E–C– groups ( p  value = 0.006,  t  test). Also, not totally
L349: unexpected, the growth rate (median body mass/age) of fish adequate
L350: in both vitamins (E+C+ group) was approximately three times higher
L351: compared with the group deficient in both vitamins (E–C–
L352: group). Vitamin C deficiency seemed to have a more drastic effect
L353: on the growth rate compared with inadequate vitamin E levels ( Supporting Information , Figure S3B).
L354: Optimization of Sample Preparation and Analytical Workflow for
L355: the Comparative “Bottom-up” Proteomics Study
L356: To account for differences in fish body weights, we normalized the
L357: total protein concentration, which resulted in solutions with equal
L358: total concentration of proteins independent of fish weights, age,
L359: and vitamin supplementation. The total protein concentration in each
L360: sample was verified to confirm the validity of the sample preparation
L361: technique and for further use. The median total concentration of proteins
L362: for all samples was 2.382 mg/mL with first quartile 2.258 mg/mL and
L363: third quartile 2.478 mg/mL. As was expected from the sample preparation
L364: that accounted for the differences in fish weights, the total concentration
L365: of proteins was distributed in a narrow range ( Supporting Information , Figure S4). After lysis, insoluble
L366: pellets that remained in the microcentrifuge tubes were discarded.
L367: The supernatant was used for protein digestion.
L368: Because of the
L369: complexity of the whole fish protein extracts, we optimized the LC
L370: conditions to increase the number of peptides detected. 49  For optimization, a protein extract was run
L371: using different LC gradients, and the number of eluted peptides was
L372: compared. By changing the mobile phase composition over time, the
L373: gradient was optimized after each run to make the elution of peptides
L374: even across a chromatogram and to reduce the coelution of peptides.
L375: The gradients were named as “Gradient 1”, “Gradient
L376: 2”, “Gradient 3”, and “Gradient 4”.
L377: The gradient profiles and respective chromatograms are presented in
L378: the  Supporting Information , Figure S5.
L379: Using Venn diagrams, a comparison of the number of eluted peptides
L380: for each gradient is presented in the  Supporting
L381: Information , Figure S6. “Gradient 4” resulted
L382: in the highest number of peptides detected and was used for all following
L383: LC–IM–MS E  analyses.
L384: Data Analysis Strategy Used for Obtaining Protein Abundance
L385: Estimates
L386: Label-free approaches are liable to technical variability,
L387: such as LC retention time drift, nanospray instability, and sample
L388: matrix effects. Therefore, to overcome these caveats, label-free approaches
L389: depend on the use of an internal standard and the application of proper
L390: mathematical tools for data processing. Log transformation of data
L391: is commonly used to make the distribution of protein abundances more
L392: Gaussian, with subsequent application of parametric statistical tests,
L393: which have higher statistical power after data transformation. 50
L394: In this study, a digest of enolase from  Saccharomyces cerevisiae  was used as an internal standard.
L395: The same volume of the internal standard was added to each sample,
L396: making its concentration equal across samples. Data were acquired,
L397: deisotoped, peak-aligned, and exported to Scaffold for quantitative
L398: analysis. The quantification of proteins was done by normalization
L399: of the protein abundances to the internal standard. For the total
L400: of 2956 quantitative values obtained, a graph of protein amounts of
L401: the identified proteins from all samples against protein ranks was
L402: created (Figure  2 ). The value for the detection
L403: limit was extracted as the lowest amount of a protein that was calculated
L404: by Scaffold. The detection limit of this method was as low as 20 amol
L405: (Heat shock protein 8, sample E+C+#5), while the highest detected
L406: concentration was 2.3 pmol (Myosin light chain, sample E–C+#8).
L407: The dynamic range of estimates of protein amounts was calculated to
L408: be about five orders of magnitude.
L409: Figure 2 Dynamic range of the method based on protein abundance estimates.
L410: The plot has the predicted S-shape, and the quantitative dynamic range
L411: covers five orders of magnitude based on 2956 data points derived
L412: from LC–IM–MS E  data of all 37 samples. The
L413: value for the detection limit was identified as the lowest amount
L414: of a protein calculated by Scaffold software. The detection limit
L415: of this method was as low as 20 amol (Heat shock protein 8, sample
L416: E+C+#5), while the highest detected amount was 2.3 pmol (Myosin light
L417: chain, sample E–C+#8).
L418: Next, data were exported to Perseus and log 2 -transformed.
L419: To estimate the biological variation between fish samples, we calculated
L420: the coefficient of determination,  R 2 ,
L421: based on the protein estimates between biological replicates. As an
L422: example,  Supporting Information  Figure
L423: S7 shows the correlation plot between the biological replicates E–C+
L424: #9 and E–C+ #8 resulting in a  R 2  value of 0.654. Additional correlation plots for the E–C+
L425: group are shown in  Supporting Information  Figure S8. As expected, variation between samples was observed.
L426: The correlation of protein amounts between different fish (within
L427: one feeding group) was found to be stronger in samples containing
L428: higher numbers of proteins.
L429: Proteome Changes Linked to Vitamin C Deficiency
L430: To
L431: investigate the proteome changes caused by vitamin C deficiency in
L432: vitamin-E-adequate fish, we evaluated the fold changes of the protein
L433: estimates obtained for the E+C+ versus the E+C– group. For
L434: quantitative analysis, peptide identifications were accepted if they
L435: could be established at a >95% probability. Protein identifications
L436: were accepted if they could be established at >99.0% probability and
L437: contained at least two identified peptides. The quantitative values
L438: calculated for the comparison of groups C+ versus C– (both
L439: sufficient in vitamin E) were based on 8134 spectra with peptide FDR
L440: 0.1% and 203 protein with protein FDR 2.0%. Next, internal standard
L441: and contaminants were removed and quantitation filter was applied.
L442: All proteins that were not detected at least two times across a group
L443: were filtered out. This conservative approach resulted in 119 proteins.
L444: Among them, 61% were up-regulated in vitamin-C-deficient fish (72
L445: of 119). A change in concentration of two-fold or more was observed
L446: for 28% of the proteins in this group.
L447: The same quantitative
L448: analysis was done to investigate the proteome changes caused by vitamin
L449: C deficiency in vitamin-E-deficient fish. For quantitative analysis,
L450: peptide identifications were accepted if they could be established
L451: at a >50% probability. Protein identifications were accepted if they
L452: could be established at >99.9% probability and contained at least
L453: two identified peptides. The quantitative values calculated for the
L454: comparison of the C+ versus C– group (both deficient in vitamin
L455: E) were based on 11 308 spectra with peptide FDR 0.2% and 221
L456: protein with protein FDR 2.3%. Again, internal standard and contaminants
L457: were removed and quantitation filter was applied. This resulted in
L458: a list of 112 proteins (Supplementary Table S3 in the  Supporting Information ). Among them, 52% were
L459: up-regulated in vitamin-C-deficient fish (58 of 112). A change in
L460: concentration of two-fold or more was observed for 29% of the proteins.
L461: Figure  3 A shows a volcano plot for the distribution
L462: of  p  values versus fold-changes calculated for the
L463: proteins that were assigned for the comparison of the E+C+ versus
L464: E+C– groups (E+C+/E+C– transition). A negative logarithm
L465: of the  p  values and the logarithm of the ratio between
L466: amount of proteins from E+C+ and E+C– groups are displayed
L467: on the  y  and  x  axes, respectively.
L468: Dashed lines show a cutoff of two-fold change and a  p -value threshold of 0.05 to define differential regulation of proteins
L469: between groups. Four proteins, namely, type-II cytokeratin, alpha
L470: tropomyosin, myosin heavy polypeptide 6, and fatty acid binding protein
L471: 6, showed a significant change upon vitamin C deficiency in vitamin-E-adequate
L472: fish, and all were upregulated. Regulation of alpha tropomyosin by
L473: α-tocopherol aligns well with previously published data. 51
L474: Figure 3 Volcano plot representation of protein abundance changes caused
L475: by vitamin C deficiency. Side-by-side comparison of the volcano plots
L476: of protein abundance changes reveals the permissive role of vitamin
L477: E in vitamin-C-deficient fish. In the group low in vitamin E, the
L478: lack of vitamin C causes changes in proteins associated with pathways
L479: of energy metabolism (pyruvate kinase and glutamate dehydrogenase)
L480: and other protein with roles in stress response.
L481: In the Figure  3 B, the volcano plot is shown
L482: for evaluating differential regulation of proteins in group E–C+
L483: versus group E–C– (E–C+/E–C– transition).
L484: We found that three proteins were down-regulated (pyruvate kinase
L485: M2b, fatty acid binding protein 11, and peptidyl-prolyl cis–trans
L486: isomerase) and six up-regulated (glial fibrillary acidic protein,
L487: ventricular myosin heavy chain, glutamate dehydrogenase 1, heat shock
L488: protein 90 kDa alpha, LOC100002040 protein, and novel myosin family
L489: protein CH211-158M24.10-001) as a consequence of vitamin C deficiency
L490: in vitamin-E-deficient fish.
L491: A side-by-side comparison of the two volcano plots reveals the
L492: permissive role of vitamin E in the effect of vitamin C deficiency
L493: on the fish. In the vitamin-E-deficient fish the additional lack of
L494: vitamin C causes changes in expression levels of proteins responsible
L495: for energy metabolism, namely, pyruvate kinase and glutamate dehydrogenase,
L496: along with other marker proteins. To obtain functional insight into
L497: proteins and pathways that are affected by vitamin C deficiency, we
L498: functionally annotated the combined proteomic data sets of the E–C–
L499: and E–C+ groups using the Gene Ontology (GO) classification
L500: ( Supporting Information , Table S4).
L501: Responsive Protein Interaction Network of Vitamin C Deficiency
L502: A protein interaction network was constructed to provide a comprehensive
L503: visualization of the proteins identified, their abundance estimates,
L504: as well as differential regulation caused by vitamin C deficiency
L505: in fish low on vitamin E ( Supporting Information , Figure S9). In the network, each node represents a protein found
L506: in either group E–C+ or E–C–. The change in protein
L507: amounts between vitamin-C-adequate and -deficient fish is color coded:
L508: green nodes represent proteins that increased their amounts, red represent
L509: those that decreased, and gray nodes show proteins that were not quantified.
L510: The size of a node represents an average of the protein abundance
L511: estimates in the E–C– group. The width of a line connecting
L512: proteins represents the strength of proteins interaction, as extracted
L513: from STRING. Figure  4  provides a closer look
L514: at subnetworks that encompassed proteins that were differentially
L515: regulated in response to vitamin E and C deficiency. Upregulated proteins
L516: were members of the subnetworks representing glutaminolysis, TCA cycle,
L517: and stress response.
L518: Figure 4 Protein–protein interaction network visualization of subnetworks
L519: that contain proteins that showed changes in expression levels upon
L520: vitamin C deficiency in the vitamin-E-deficient state. In the network
L521: each node represents a protein found in either group E–C+ or
L522: E–C–. The change in protein amounts between vitamin-C-adequate
L523: and -deficient fish is color-coded: green nodes represent proteins
L524: that increased their amounts, red represents those that decreased;
L525: gray nodes show proteins that were not quantified. The size of a node
L526: represents an average of the protein abundance estimates in the E+C–
L527: group. The width of a line connecting proteins represents the strength
L528: of proteins interaction, as extracted from STRING software. Subnetworks
L529: of proteins involved in glycolysis and the TCA cycle are highlighted
L530: as well as proteins associated with stress response.
L531: Biological Inferences of Altered Protein Expressions in Vitamin-E-
L532: and -C-Deficient Zebrafish
L533: The interaction network highlights
L534: the modulation of several proteins involved in two interconnected
L535: metabolic pathways, the glycolysis and TCA cycle (Figure  5 ). Proteins are shown as upregulated (green arrow
L536: up), downregulated (red arrow down), unquantified (gray arrow), and
L537: not detected (no arrow) with their respective numerical fold changes
L538: ( Supporting Information , Table S4). Proteins
L539: that showed a statistically significant fold change are marked with
L540: an asterisk.
L541: Figure 5 Schematic of the metabolic pathways that were associated with proteins
L542: that showed changes in abundance estimates as a consequence of vitamin
L543: C deficiency. To analyze the consequences of vitamin C deficiency,
L544: we combined quantitative data with metabolic pathways linked to energy
L545: production, namely, glycolysis, TCA cycle, and glutaminolysis. Proteins
L546: that were upregulated are indicated by a green arrow up, downregulated
L547: proteins are marked with a red arrow down, detected but not quantified
L548: proteins are marked with a double arrow in gray, and not detected
L549: proteins are unmarked. Proteins are labeled with their respective
L550: numerical fold-changes observed for vitamin C deficiency (in the vitamin-E-deficient
L551: group). Proteins that showed a statistically significant fold change
L552: are marked with an asterisk.
L553: Upon vitamin C deficiency in groups low on vitamin E, glycolytic
L554: enzymes were downregulated. The last step in the glycolysis pathway
L555: involves a transfer of the phosphate group from phosphoenolpyruvate
L556: to ADP, producing a molecule of pyruvate and a molecule of ATP. This
L557: step is catalyzed by pyruvate kinase (PK), which was significantly
L558: downregulated in vitamin-E- and -C-deficient fish ( p  value = 0.009,  t  test for log-transformed data).
L559: In humans and other mammals, four isoforms of pyruvate kinase are
L560: expressed: the L and R isoforms are found in liver and red blood cells,
L561: the M1 isoform is expressed in most adult tissues, and the M2 isoform,
L562: a splice variant of M1, is the predominant form found in proliferating
L563: cells and tumor cells. 52 , 53  In the current study, pyruvate
L564: kinase M2 was detected but not M1 nor L/R. Western Blot analysis confirmed
L565: the presence and change of pyruvate kinase M2 (data not shown). Under
L566: the conditions of suppressed glycolysis there will be less production
L567: of energy (ATP) and reducing power (NADH), the main products of the
L568: cycle. We may therefore speculate that in vitamin-E- and -C-deficient
L569: fish compensatory mechanisms for the production of energy are activated.
L570: Kirkwood et al. studied vitamin-C-deficient zebrafish at the metabolome
L571: level and reported activation of the purine nucleotide cycle as possible
L572: compensatory mechanism to satisfy intracellular ATP requirements. 29
L573: After conversion of glutamine to glutamate, glutamate dehydrogenase
L574: catalyzes the production of α-ketoglutarate, which subsequently
L575: enters the TCA cycle. The conversion of glutamine to lactate is commonly
L576: referred as glutaminolysis. 54 , 55  Our quantitative proteomic
L577: study indicates that several proteins that play key roles in glutaminolysis
L578: are elevated in vitamin-E- and -C-deficient fish (E–C–
L579: group). Our data sets show a significant increase in levels of glutamate
L580: dehydrogenase (Glud1b) under vitamin E and C deficiency compared with
L581: E deficiency alone ( p  value = 0.046,  t  test for log-transformed data). The change in this protein was confirmed
L582: with Western Blot (data not shown). Malate dehydrogenase and lactate
L583: dehydrogenase, enzymes involved in glutaminolysis, were increased
L584: as well. The elevation of several key enzymes involved in glutaminolysis
L585: supports the notion that vitamin E and C deficiency promotes a metabolic
L586: phenotype in which glutaminolytic pathways are activated for alternative
L587: energy production (ATP) and reducing power (NADPH) in cells under
L588: conditions of suppressed glycolysis.
L589: In addition, our quantitative proteomics screens revealed that
L590: Glial fibrillary acidic protein (Gfap) was significantly elevated
L591: (27 times) in fish deficient in both vitamins ( p  value
L592: = 0.002,  t  test for log transformed data), while
L593: there was no significant difference in Gfap expression between E+C+
L594: and E–C+ groups consistent with the notion that vitamin E seems
L595: to exhibit a permissive role in governing the proteome biology of
L596: adult zebrafish. The adverse effect of deficiency of both vitamins
L597: has been previously reported. 2  The identification
L598: of Gfap was based on a conservative probability for protein identification
L599: of at least 85% and at least two unique peptides with peptide identification
L600: probability of at least 50%. The similarity between zebrafish and
L601: human Gfap (GFAP_HUMAN, entry P14136 in Uniprot) is 65%, as calculated
L602: by Blast search. Despite the good homology of the human and zebrafish
L603: protein, the human antibody used in the Western blot analyses showed
L604: nonspecific binding and Western blots were not conclusive. Gfap is
L605: a recognized marker of neurologic injury and trauma and is released
L606: during astrogliosis in higher vertebrates. 56 − 58
L607: Conclusions
L608: We applied a label-free comparative proteomics approach for examining
L609: the systems-wide consequences of insufficient levels of two essential
L610: micronutrients, vitamins E and C, in adult zebrafish. The experimental
L611: design adopted allowed the study of vitamin C deficiency at the whole
L612: organism level. Using a label-free quantitative proteomic workflow,
L613: it was possible to assess for the first time changes in the vertebrate
L614: proteome upon vitamin C deficiency. The label-free quantitative bottom-up
L615: strategy with LC–ion mobility–MS E  is a powerful
L616: approach to determine protein abundance estimates in zebrafish. We
L617: report sensitivity of the method as low as 20 amol and a dynamic range
L618: of five orders of magnitude for protein level estimates. Our results
L619: indicate the modulation of expression of proteins involved in stress
L620: response and metabolic pathways associated with energy production.
L621: Our findings suggest that severe vitamin C deficiency potentiated
L622: by vitamin E deficiency causes the suppression of glycolysis and the
L623: activation of glutaminolysis as an alternative way to fulfill cellular
L624: energy requirements. Glial fibrillary acidic protein (Gfap) showed
L625: a significant overexpression in fish low in both vitamins C and E,
L626: proving that severe vitamin C deficiency in combination with low vitamin
L627: E status causes injury of the central nervous system.
