L820: **Supplementary Table S1.** Pipetting scheme used for sample lysis and preparation of digests.
L821: **Supplementary Table S2.** Synapt G2 HDMS instrument settings used for Ion-Mobility-MS [E]
L822: acquisitions.
L823: **Supplementary Table S3.** The number of peptides and proteins found in each of the biological
L824: samples subjected to LC-IM-MS [E] .
L825: **Supplementary Table S4.** Proteins identified in the E-C+ and the E-C- groups, GO annotations
L826: and fold-changes observed in the vitamin C deficient fish (E-C-/E-C+ transition).
L827: **Excel file: Supplementary Table S5.** Protein Table for Study (A) E+C+ vs E+C- and (B) E-C+
L828: vs E-C- The compilation of proteins found in each samples, unique peptides identified per
L829: protein and respective sequence coverage.
L830: **Supplementary Figure S1** . Design of the feeding experiment. Briefly, at 42 day of age, 40 fish
L831: were divided in two groups and fed diets low in vitamin C with vitamin E at sufficient or
L832: deficient levels. Thus, two vitamin groups were created: E+C- and E-C- with 20 fish in each
L833: group. Supplementation continued for 56 days, after which half of the fish population in each
L834: group was harvested. The diet of remaining fish was altered to have a high amount of vitamin C,
L835: thus creating another two vitamin groups: E+C+ and E-C+. After an additional 21 days, the fish
L836: were harvested. Thus, four groups with 10 fish in each were created, each having different
L837: vitamins levels that varied with supplementation: E+C+, E-C+, E+C- and E-C-.
L838: **Supplemental Figure S2** . Workflow used for preparing the samples for the label-free “bottom
L839: up” quantitative proteomics study.
L840: Fish was individually ground under liquid nitrogen using a mortar and pestle. While still dry, the
L841: fish powder was transferred to a pre-weighted microcentrifuge tube and the mass of the tube with
L842: fish powder was recorded. The mass of the fish was calculated by the difference. A PBS solution
L843: with ProteaseMAX (0.04%) was added to the micro tubes for the extraction of proteins; its
L844: volume was adjusted to account for fish mass variability. Assuming the density of extraction
L845: solution 1 mg/mL, its mass was calculated as 6 times that of a fish. Tubes of fish powder solution
L846: were frozen in liquid nitrogen for 2 minutes, thawed for 5 min, and sonicated for 15 minutes.
L847: Last three steps were repeated three times. After lysis, tubes were centrifuged at 4 ºC for 10
L848: minutes at 15,000 relative centrifugal force (rcf) and the supernatants were transferred to new
L849: microcentrifuge tubes (1.3 mL). After protein digestion, 9  L of digest was mixed with 1 µL of
L850: the internal standard and the mixture was subjected to LC- ion mobility – MS [E] analysis.
L851: **Supplementary Figure S3** . Fish characteristics dependent on the vitamin regime fed.
L852: (A) Boxplots of distribution of fish body weight. Fish from vitamin groups were harvested at
L853: preselected age (98 days vs. 119 days) and had a different vitamin supplementation.
L854: Therefore it was expected that there will be a difference between the average fish mass
L855: between groups. The median fish mass is lower for groups with a shorter life span, but the
L856: difference was statistically significant only between E+C+ and E-C- groups (p-value=0.006).
L857: (B) The ratios of median growth rate between vitamins groups. Ratios representing the effect of
L858: vitamin E deficiency are colored in green and those representing the effect of vitamin C are
L859: in blue. The growth rate (median body mass / age) of fish adequate in both vitamins (E+C+
L860: group) was approximately 3-times higher compared to the group deficient in both vitamins
L861: (E-C- group). Vitamin C deficiency seemed to have a more drastic effect on the growth rate
L862: compared to inadequate vitamin E levels.
L863: **Supplemental Figure S4** . A boxplot of the distribution of the total protein concentration after
L864: extraction and lysis.
L865: Total protein concentrations were distributed in a narrow range, as was expected with the sample
L866: preparation that accounts for differences in fish mass. The median of total protein concentration
L867: was 2.382 mg/mL, sample size=37.
L868: **Supplementary Figure S5.** Gradient profiles with respective base peak chromatograms.
L869: To yield the maximum number of eluted peptides, a LC gradient was optimized. For this, a
L870: sample was run using different LC gradients and the number of eluted peptides was compared.
L871: By changing the mobile phase composition over time, the gradient was optimized after each run
L872: to make the elution of peptides even across a chromatogram and to reduce peptide coelution. The
L873: gradients were named “Gradient1”, “Gradient2”, “Gradient3” and “Gradient4”. The comparison
L874: of the total ion chromatograms illustrates how different clusters of eluting compounds are
L875: moving across the chromatogram allowing better separation of peptides dependent on the
L876: gradient design.
L877: **Supplementary Figure S6** . Venn’s diagrams comparing the number of peptides eluting from a
L878: column and identified with LC-IM-MS [E] .
L879: More identified peptides means more identified proteins with greater probability. Gradient 4 (3%
L880: to 40% B in 90 minutes) was chosen for further peptide separation.
L881: **Supplementary Figure S7** . Correlation plot between E-C+ sample 9 and E-C+ sample 8.
L882: On the axis is log2 of the concentration of proteins found in both groups. 95% confidence band is
L883: drawn with short-dashed line, 95% prediction band is long-dashed line. Adjusted R [2] shows some
L884: variability between biological replicates.
L885: **Supplementary Figure S8** . Correlation plot matrix of the E-C+ group.
L886: As expected, variation between fish was observed and the correlation is stronger between
L887: samples, where the number of proteins found is higher.
L888: **Supplementary Figure S9** . The responsive protein network for the vitamin transition C+/C- and
L889: both groups deficient in vitamin E status (E-C+ vs E-C+). The network was constructed using
L890: proteins observed in the quantitative proteomics screens of fish adequate in vitamin C versus fish
L891: deficient in vitamin C both with deficient vitamin E status (transition E-C+ /E-C- ). In the
L892: network each node represents a protein found in either group E-C+ or E-C-. The change in
L893: protein amounts between vitamin C adequate and deficient fish is color coded: green nodes
L894: represent proteins that increased their amounts, red represent those that decreased, and grey
L895: nodes show proteins that were not quantified. The size of a node represents an average of the
L896: protein abundance estimate in E-C- group. The width of a line connecting proteins represents the
L897: strength of proteins interaction, as extracted from STRING. Proteins involved in glycolysis, the
L898: TCA cycle, stress response and heat shock proteins are highlighted.
